1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,975
Hi, I'm Dr. Itan H. Ruiz Hernandez.

2
00:00:01,975 --> 00:00:04,063
This project starts with a real case:

3
00:00:04,063 --> 00:00:06,434
a child with Mosaic Variegated Aneuploidy,

4
00:00:06,434 --> 00:00:09,030
an ultra-rare disease with no treatment today.

5
00:00:09,030 --> 00:00:11,400
In Track 1, we reached a genetic diagnosis

6
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:13,037
that's very likely the cause.

7
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:16,423
This part, Track 2, is about what we do with that diagnosis.

8
00:00:16,423 --> 00:00:19,245
We found two already-approved drugs worth trying —

9
00:00:19,245 --> 00:00:21,333
not because we picked them at random,

10
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:25,170
but because we understood exactly how this disease damages the cell.

11
00:00:25,170 --> 00:00:30,579
The child has both copies of the BUB1B gene damaged.

12
00:00:30,579 --> 00:00:36,715
One variant is already registered in ClinVar as pathogenic.

13
00:00:36,715 --> 00:00:40,356
The other is new, not reported yet,

14
00:00:40,356 --> 00:00:45,036
but SIFT and PolyPhen predict it as damaging.

15
00:00:45,036 --> 00:00:48,156
BUB1B makes the BUBR1 protein,

16
00:00:48,156 --> 00:00:53,045
a key piece of the mitotic spindle checkpoint —

17
00:00:53,045 --> 00:00:58,141
basically the system that checks every chromosome

18
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:02,926
is properly connected before the cell divides.

19
00:01:02,926 --> 00:01:07,043
When the amount of BUBR1 drops,

20
00:01:07,043 --> 00:01:12,224
that checkpoint stops working properly.

21
00:01:12,224 --> 00:01:16,341
Chromosomes separate too early;

22
00:01:16,341 --> 00:01:23,514
the cell ends up with the wrong number of chromosomes,

23
00:01:23,514 --> 00:01:31,085
and over time that instability predisposes these children

24
00:01:31,085 --> 00:01:35,468
to cancers like rhabdomyosarcoma.

25
00:01:35,468 --> 00:01:41,711
We also checked this in STRING and in Reactome,

26
00:01:41,711 --> 00:01:44,766
two separate databases,

27
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:50,477
and in both, BUB1B sits right at the center

28
00:01:50,477 --> 00:01:53,266
of this same pathway.

29
00:01:53,266 --> 00:01:58,032
So for Track 2 the question wasn't how do we fix the checkpoint,

30
00:01:58,032 --> 00:01:59,820
but something different:

31
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:03,022
if this cell already has a weak checkpoint,

32
00:02:03,022 --> 00:02:06,224
what approved drug takes advantage of that?

33
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:07,192
We found two.

34
00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:09,352
Bosutinib, used for leukemia,

35
00:02:09,352 --> 00:02:13,298
was already shown in a study to be specifically toxic

36
00:02:13,298 --> 00:02:16,128
to cells with a defective checkpoint —

37
00:02:16,128 --> 00:02:17,766
exactly the case here.

38
00:02:17,766 --> 00:02:22,979
And metformin, which you probably know because it's used for diabetes,

39
00:02:22,979 --> 00:02:27,224
in a 2011 study published in Cell by Tang and colleagues,

40
00:02:27,224 --> 00:02:32,064
showed that it selectively stresses aneuploid cells through AMPK.

41
00:02:32,064 --> 00:02:36,384
Both are cheap, generic drugs with years of use in humans,

42
00:02:36,384 --> 00:02:39,437
which matters a lot for a disease so rare

43
00:02:39,437 --> 00:02:43,533
that nobody is going to develop a new drug just for it.

44
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:46,511
And something we deliberately ruled out:

45
00:02:46,511 --> 00:02:50,309
checkpoint inhibitors being developed for oncology.

46
00:02:50,309 --> 00:02:52,916
Those go in the opposite direction,

47
00:02:52,916 --> 00:02:56,192
because here the checkpoint is already weak.

48
00:02:56,192 --> 00:02:57,841
And we didn't stop at theory.

49
00:02:57,841 --> 00:03:00,968
We checked whether bosutinib actually binds its target,

50
00:03:00,968 --> 00:03:01,651
and it does:

51
00:03:01,651 --> 00:03:03,357
a crystal structure, PDB 4MXO,

52
00:03:03,357 --> 00:03:05,574
of bosutinib bound to human Src kinase.

53
00:03:05,574 --> 00:03:08,474
It's the same mechanism Schukken described in 2020.

54
00:03:08,474 --> 00:03:10,862
Another structure shows it also binds Abl.

55
00:03:10,862 --> 00:03:12,795
This isn't a computer prediction —

56
00:03:12,795 --> 00:03:15,070
it's already been proven experimentally.

57
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:17,950
And this doesn't apply only to this mutation.

58
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:20,125
The other two genes that cause MVA

59
00:03:20,125 --> 00:03:21,725
reach the same mechanism,

60
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:24,797
and the same vulnerability has already been seen

61
00:03:24,797 --> 00:03:27,100
in glioblastoma and prostate cancer.

62
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:31,004
The path we followed — phenotype, mechanism, approved drugs —

63
00:03:31,004 --> 00:03:33,948
can be repeated for the next undiagnosed case.

64
00:03:33,948 --> 00:03:36,585
These are hypotheses, not proven treatments.

65
00:03:36,585 --> 00:03:38,682
The next step would be testing them

66
00:03:38,682 --> 00:03:40,300
in the patient's own cells.

67
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:43,596
But for a family that recently had no diagnosis at all,

68
00:03:43,596 --> 00:03:46,533
we believe this is already a real starting point.

